@37:50 this is not a great question as it is leading the AI to find an issue.
The hallucinations that occur when you ask "What is wrong with the object in the image?" increase dramatically because you are implying there is something wrong (ie: it'll find something wrong with an object that isn't wrong more often now) when you ask a question in this leading way. Instead (just like in survey questioning of humans) they need to take a more neutral position as the prompt, otherwise it'll find something wrong with every single object in the production line.
This suffers from the same issue as that Google employee who kept asking leading questions and thinking the responses proved intelligence.

People seated at the table? 11, 12, or 13. Head count (11), arm count (12), dish count (11 to 13 depending on whether the people associated with the extra two dishes were sitting or not.)

Call me when somebody gets this one right to this degree.

{^_-}
